        <h2>What to Buy: Tea as a Gift</h2>

        <p>Ceylon tea is one of the most universally appreciated gifts you can bring home from Sri Lanka. For quality loose-leaf tea, the best shops are directly at the estates (Mackwoods, Pedro, Dambatenne) or at specialist tea merchants in Colombo. The Dilmah factory shop in Colombo sells the full range at factory prices. Avoid buying tea from tourist shops in Ella or Galle Fort — the quality is inconsistent and the prices inflated.</p>

        <p>What to look for: estate-specific tea from a named region (Nuwara Eliya, Dimbula, or Uva), packed in a foil-sealed bag to preserve freshness. For gifts, presentation tins from Mackwoods, Mlesna or Dilmah are widely available and travel well. For your own drinking, 200–500g of loose-leaf BOP from a Nuwara Eliya estate is the single best thing you can bring home.</p>

        <h2>The Tea Train: Kandy to Ella</h2>

        <p>The train journey from Kandy through Nuwara Eliya to Ella — passing through the heart of Sri Lanka's tea country — is consistently voted one of the most scenic train journeys in the world. The route climbs through mist-shrouded highlands, past emerald tea terraces, through tunnels and over bridges that offer vertiginous views of the surrounding valleys. The journey from Kandy to Ella takes approximately 7 hours on the slow train (the most scenic option) or 5 hours on the intercity express. Book early: the observation car seats sell out weeks in advance during peak season.</p>

        <p>A cup of tea from the train vendor — strong, sweet, served in a small cup — drunk while watching the tea fields pass outside the window is one of those small travel experiences that turns out to be entirely as good as it sounds.</p>

